\par
\b 4. OOP Concepts Used In Code\b0\par
\b Encapsulation:\b0\par
Book, User, Rating, and repository classes keep data private and expose controlled getters and setters.\par
\par
\b STL set Usage:\b0\par
User stores preferred genres, preferred authors, and read books using set containers so duplicate entries are prevented automatically and the output stays ordered.\par
\par
\b Abstraction:\b0\par
IRecommendationStrategy and BookFilter define abstract behavior without exposing implementation details.\par
\par
\b Inheritance:\b0\par
ContentBasedStrategy, CollaborativeStrategy, and HybridStrategy inherit from IRecommendationStrategy. GenreFilter, MinYearFilter, and MinRatingFilter inherit from BookFilter. DataAccessException and ValidationException inherit from ProjectException.\par
\par
\b Polymorphism / Virtual Function:\b0\par
RecommendationEngine stores an IRecommendationStrategy pointer and calls recommend() and name() dynamically at runtime. FilterChain stores BookFilter objects and calls matches() through the base interface.\par
\par
\b Operator Overloading:\b0\par
Book overloads operator<, operator==, and operator<< for comparison and formatted output.\par
\par
\b Templates:\b0\par
SearchService::sortBooks uses a template comparator. FilterChain::addFilter also uses a template so different filter types can be created safely with one reusable function.\par
\par
\b Exception Handling:\b0\par
CSVParser throws DataAccessException for file problems. Book throws ValidationException for invalid ratings. MenuController catches ProjectException and shows a friendly runtime message.\par
\par
\b Beautification / Formatted Output:\b0\par
The menu uses iomanip functions such as setw and setprecision to print aligned book tables during demonstration.\par
\par
\b 5. Likely Viva Questions And Answers\b0\par
\b Q: Why did you use an abstract class for recommendation?\b0\par
A: We wanted the engine to support multiple algorithms without changing the engine itself. The abstract interface makes the system extensible and supports runtime polymorphism.\par
\par
\b Q: If the final system uses one logic, why keep multiple strategy classes?\b0\par
A: The final product uses hybrid recommendation only, but the design still keeps the architecture extensible. This shows abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism clearly in code.\par
\par
\b Q: Where is polymorphism used?\b0\par
A: In RecommendationEngine, the active strategy is stored as an IRecommendationStrategy pointer. The engine calls recommend() through the base interface, and the HybridStrategy object handles the actual implementation at runtime.\par
\par
\b Q: Where is inheritance used?\b0\par
A: The strategy classes inherit from IRecommendationStrategy, filter classes inherit from BookFilter, and custom exception classes inherit from ProjectException.\par
\par
\b Q: Why did you use templates?\b0\par
A: Templates let us write reusable, type-safe logic. sortBooks works with any comparator, and addFilter can construct any filter type without duplicating code.\par
\par
\b Q: How is exception handling useful here?\b0\par
A: It separates normal logic from error handling. File and validation errors are thrown at the source and handled centrally in the menu layer.\par
\par
\b Q: How can Abrar explain his contribution clearly?\b0\par
A: Abrar should explain the flow from main.cpp to RecommendationEngine, then explain the strategy interface, then explain how the final HybridStrategy produces recommendations using recommendation data.\par
\par
\b 6. System Design Talking Points\b0\par
1. Repository layer separates data access from business logic.\par
2. RecommendationEngine coordinates recommendation execution but does not hardcode all algorithm details in one place.\par
3. SearchService handles searching, sorting, and filtering.\par
4. Entity classes such as Book and User encapsulate domain rules.\par
5. MenuController manages user interaction and exception-safe execution flow.\par
\par
\b 7. What We Could Have Improved But Failed To Finish\b0\par
1. Stronger password security using hashing instead of plain-text storage.\par
2. Better persistence with a database instead of CSV files.\par
3. Unit tests for recommendation scoring and repository validation.\par
4. Smarter recommendation evaluation metrics and feedback tracking.\par
5. Better UI, including pagination and richer book details.\par
6. More advanced personalization and feedback learning from user behavior.\par
